### Writes `new` if the value here is `expected`, atomically and sequentially consistent, and returns whether it did. The value that was here is stored through `previous` either way. It may also fail when the value matched (a spurious failure), so it belongs in a retry loop. The failure order is sequentially consistent.
### :typeparam T: The pointee type, which must support the operation atomically on the target.
### :param expected: The value the memory is expected to hold.
### :param new: The value to write when it matches.
### :param previous: Receives the value that was in memory.
### :returns: True when the exchange happened.
@external("llvm")
@template("{t0} = cmpxchg weak ptr {me}, {T} {expected}, {T} {new} seq_cst seq_cst, align {sizeof T}\n{t1} = extractvalue {{ {T}, i1 }} {t0}, 0\nstore {T} {t1}, ptr {previous}\n{result} = extractvalue {{ {T}, i1 }} {t0}, 1")
routine Ptr[T].atomic_compare_exchange_weak_raw(me: Me, expected: T, new: T, previous: Me) -> Bool
needs T: typename, AtomicExchangeable[T]

### Writes new if the value here is expected, atomically and sequentially consistent: None if it did,
### the value found if not. It may also fail when the value matched (a spurious failure), so it belongs in a retry loop, where it is cheaper on LL/SC machines (ARM, AArch64, RISC-V).
